{
  "case_id": "2006-06-531",
  "document_hash": "4981c3b5eb6c16713c8bcc0f03c45df7bf8f8e070e9cdb4f98aa9cdce58729fd",
  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2006-06-531",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "4981c3b5eb6c16713c8bcc0f03c45df7bf8f8e070e9cdb4f98aa9cdce58729fd",
      "evidence_id": "ev-4981c3b5eb6c-0000-0000",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 1,
        "start": 1
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The above-entitled matter came on for oral argument before the Supreme Court of the United States at 11:20 a.m."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioners",
      "case_id": "2006-06-531",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "4981c3b5eb6c16713c8bcc0f03c45df7bf8f8e070e9cdb4f98aa9cdce58729fd",
      "evidence_id": "ev-4981c3b5eb6c-0000-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 3,
        "start": 3
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The court of appeals held that plaintiffs who obtain a preliminary injunction are prevailing parties entitled to fees, even though the district court concluded that the State's nudity ban does not violate the Constitution, that plaintiffs were not entitled to any permanent relief, and that final judgment should be entered against the plaintiffs."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Chief Justice Roberts",
      "case_id": "2006-06-531",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "4981c3b5eb6c16713c8bcc0f03c45df7bf8f8e070e9cdb4f98aa9cdce58729fd",
      "evidence_id": "ev-4981c3b5eb6c-0000-0005",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 3,
        "start": 3
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "CHIEF JUSTICE ROBERTS: We'll next hear Case 06-531, Sole versus Wyner."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "United States",
      "case_id": "2006-06-531",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "4981c3b5eb6c16713c8bcc0f03c45df7bf8f8e070e9cdb4f98aa9cdce58729fd",
      "evidence_id": "ev-4981c3b5eb6c-0000-0002",
      "kind": "amicus_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 18,
        "start": 18
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "This Court has held repeatedly that liability for attorney's fees and liability for violation of Federal law go hand in hand. A preliminary injunction does not determine that there has been a violation of Federal law."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Ms. Millett",
      "case_id": "2006-06-531",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "4981c3b5eb6c16713c8bcc0f03c45df7bf8f8e070e9cdb4f98aa9cdce58729fd",
      "evidence_id": "ev-4981c3b5eb6c-0001-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 21,
        "start": 21
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MS. MILLE TT: No, that's right. This\nCourt, while repeatedly stating, including unanimously\njust two years ago, that the central justification for\nattorney's fees is that the defendant is a violator of\nFederal law, has found that it will also permit\nattorney's fees in the consent decree situation, but\nthat's because there you have a defendant who is not\nfighting and continuing to resist any form of final\nrelief, has instead agreed to provide final relief that\nruns to a plaintiff, in favor of a plaintiff, and that\nadvances the purpose of Federal law."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Scalia",
      "case_id": "2006-06-531",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "4981c3b5eb6c16713c8bcc0f03c45df7bf8f8e070e9cdb4f98aa9cdce58729fd",
      "evidence_id": "ev-4981c3b5eb6c-0001-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 30,
        "start": 30
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SCALIA: What if they got a TRO\ninstead of a preliminary injunction?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. GALANTER",
      "case_id": "2006-06-531",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "4981c3b5eb6c16713c8bcc0f03c45df7bf8f8e070e9cdb4f98aa9cdce58729fd",
      "evidence_id": "ev-4981c3b5eb6c-0002-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 39,
        "start": 39
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. GALANTER: I don't believe that that's the fairest reading of the district court's subsequent orders in this case. What the district court made clear was that his as-applied holding, that the plays and the war protests were being treated differently. Remember that it's --"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Stevens",
      "case_id": "2006-06-531",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "4981c3b5eb6c16713c8bcc0f03c45df7bf8f8e070e9cdb4f98aa9cdce58729fd",
      "evidence_id": "ev-4981c3b5eb6c-0002-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 47,
        "start": 47
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE STEVENS: Let me ask you this. Is it correct that an underlying principle of law that justified your claim for relief and your actual relief was that there's some First Amendment right to demonstrate in the nude?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Souter",
      "case_id": "2006-06-531",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "4981c3b5eb6c16713c8bcc0f03c45df7bf8f8e070e9cdb4f98aa9cdce58729fd",
      "evidence_id": "ev-4981c3b5eb6c-0002-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 55,
        "start": 55
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SOUTER: Because the TRO case, characteristically the other side isn't heard, so you haven't prevailed."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Scalia",
      "case_id": "2006-06-531",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "4981c3b5eb6c16713c8bcc0f03c45df7bf8f8e070e9cdb4f98aa9cdce58729fd",
      "evidence_id": "ev-4981c3b5eb6c-0003-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 56,
        "start": 56
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Well, you surely wouldn't say that the fact that the other side never shows up means that you can't get your attorney's fees."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Stevens",
      "case_id": "2006-06-531",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "4981c3b5eb6c16713c8bcc0f03c45df7bf8f8e070e9cdb4f98aa9cdce58729fd",
      "evidence_id": "ev-4981c3b5eb6c-0003-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 56,
        "start": 56
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Yes. But in this very case, if you had gotten a TRO instead of a preliminary injunction, you'd have exactly the same practical situation."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Galanter",
      "case_id": "2006-06-531",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "4981c3b5eb6c16713c8bcc0f03c45df7bf8f8e070e9cdb4f98aa9cdce58729fd",
      "evidence_id": "ev-4981c3b5eb6c-0003-0003",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 57,
        "start": 57
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "With three attorneys, Your Honor. And yes -- and we both have our stories about why there was short notice. Ours is they only told us a week before they weren't going to allow her to protest nude."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Ginsburg",
      "case_id": "2006-06-531",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "4981c3b5eb6c16713c8bcc0f03c45df7bf8f8e070e9cdb4f98aa9cdce58729fd",
      "evidence_id": "ev-4981c3b5eb6c-0003-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 58,
        "start": 58
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Ms. Seitz, would you clarify that point about costs? Did your client have to pay costs?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Ms. Seitz",
      "case_id": "2006-06-531",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "4981c3b5eb6c16713c8bcc0f03c45df7bf8f8e070e9cdb4f98aa9cdce58729fd",
      "evidence_id": "ev-4981c3b5eb6c-0003-0004",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 59,
        "start": 59
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The plaintiffs were awarded costs incurred on the preliminary injunction. My client was awarded a right to costs on the remainder of the litigation. Those costs were reduced to mirror the precise costs that the plaintiff was awarded on preliminary injunction, so in the end no one received any costs."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Source extraction",
      "case_id": "2006-06-531",
      "confidence": 0.0,
      "document_hash": "4981c3b5eb6c16713c8bcc0f03c45df7bf8f8e070e9cdb4f98aa9cdce58729fd",
